title: 3CX / X_TRADER
page: breaches/3cx-x-trader.html
as-of: 2026-11-22
promise: Mandiant traced the intrusion behind 3CX's backdoored, validly signed softphone to a trojanized trading app on an employee's personal computer, and called it the first cascading software supply chain compromise it was aware of.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: First | cascading software supply chain compromise | that Mandiant had seen
stat: Both | build environments compromised | Windows and macOS
The 3CX softphone was used by hundreds of thousands of organizations. The entry point was an end-of-life trading app on an employee's personal computer. The actor was UNC4736, North Korea-linked and associated with AppleJeus.
notes:
Start with the figures the page leads with, and with who stands behind each one.

In March 2023, the 3CX desktop softphone, used by hundreds of thousands of organizations, was found shipping with a backdoor. A softphone is phone software that runs on a computer. Mandiant traced the intrusion back to a 3CX employee who downloaded a trojanized trading application onto a personal computer, and called it the first cascading software supply chain compromise it was aware of. Keep Mandiant's name on that claim when you say it aloud.

The actor compromised both the Windows and the macOS build environments. A build environment is where a company compiles and packages its software for release.

The page names the actor as UNC4736, a North Korea-linked cluster associated with AppleJeus.

The entry point was an end-of-life application from Trading Technologies. End of life means its vendor no longer supports it, and the page calls this one unsupported, unpatched and still downloadable.

== Found in March 2023: a backdoored 3CX softphone
layout: text
A 3CX employee ran a trojanized, end-of-life trading app on a personal computer. Mandiant assesses UNC4736 stole the employee's 3CX credentials. The actor used them over VPN and compromised both build environments, so 3CX signed and shipped a backdoored app.
notes:
The page dates the discovery: in March 2023, the 3CX desktop softphone was found shipping with a backdoor. At stage 04 it adds that EDR and antivirus vendors began flagging the updates as early as March 22, 2023, per Volexity. It does not date the steps of the intrusion, so this deck does not either.

Mandiant traced the intrusion back to a 3CX employee who downloaded X_TRADER_r7.17.90p608.exe onto a personal computer. The file was an end-of-life trading application from Trading Technologies that had itself been trojanized. Mandiant assesses that UNC4736 stole the employee's 3CX credentials from that computer.

The actor used them over VPN, a virtual private network into the company, moved laterally, and compromised both the Windows and macOS build environments. So 3CX signed and shipped a backdoored application to its own customers.

The page puts the point in one line: a supply chain attack was the delivery mechanism for a second supply chain attack.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Initial Access | Trojanized X_TRADER on a personal PC | T1195.002
step: 02 | Credential Access | Credentials, then VPN | T1078
step: 03 | Lateral Movement | Both build environments | T1021
step: 04 | Supply Chain | 3CX signs and ships it | T1553.002
step: 05 | Command and Control | Config in GitHub icons | T1102
step: 06 | Impact | The cascade | -
Arrows follow the page's stage order.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's six stages, each with its phase and primary ATT&CK technique.

Stage 01, Initial Access, is the trojanized X_TRADER installer, run on a personal computer. Stage 02, Credential Access, is the employee's 3CX credentials, which Mandiant assesses UNC4736 stole, used over VPN. Stage 03, Lateral Movement, is the move through 3CX's network into both build environments. Stage 04, which the page files under Supply Chain, is 3CX signing and shipping the backdoored app. Stage 05, Command and Control, is the configuration hidden in icon files on GitHub.

Stage 06, Impact, is the cascade itself. The page maps it to MITRE's Campaign C0057 rather than to a technique, so its box shows no ID.

The arrows follow the page's stage order. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== A 3CX employee ran trojanized X_TRADER
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1195.002 | Compromise Software Supply Chain
technique: T1204.002 | User Execution: Malicious File
fact: End of life | unsupported by Trading Technologies, still downloadable
fact: Device | a personal computer, outside 3CX's endpoint controls
fact: Relevance to work | not explained; Trading Technologies says it never supplied 3CX
fact: Payload | a modular backdoor deployed by the installer
Researchers noted the trojanized package carried a valid signature, which let it pass the checks a cautious user would run.
notes:
The page maps this stage to T1195.002, Compromise Software Supply Chain, and T1204.002, User Execution: Malicious File.

A 3CX employee downloaded and ran X_TRADER_r7.17.90p608.exe on a personal computer. It was an end-of-life Trading Technologies application that had been backdoored at source, and the installer deployed a modular backdoor.

The page says three properties stack here, and each one matters. The software was end of life, so nobody was patching, monitoring or thinking about it, and it remained downloadable long after Trading Technologies had stopped supporting it. The download happened on a personal computer, outside any endpoint control 3CX operated. And it was a trading tool from a company that, by its own account, was never a software supplier to 3CX, so no vendor risk process would ever have covered it. ReversingLabs reported that why the employee installed it remained unanswered, and that 3CX had not commented on the circumstances of the download.

Researchers noted that the trojanized package carried a valid signature, which is what let it pass the checks a cautious user would run.

The next slide gives ATT&CK's general description of a software supply chain compromise.

== What ATT&CK calls compromising the software supply chain
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1195.002 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1195/002/
ATT&CK describes adversaries manipulating application software before a final consumer receives it. It says this can happen in several ways, including changes to source code or to the update and distribution mechanism, or replacing compiled releases with modified ones.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK files this under Supply Chain Compromise: Compromise Software Supply Chain. Adversaries may manipulate application software prior to receipt by a final consumer, for the purpose of data or system compromise.

ATT&CK says this can take place in a number of ways, including manipulation of the application source code, manipulation of the update or distribution mechanism for the software, and replacing compiled releases with a modified version.

The page maps stages 01 and 03 to this technique, and its summary calls the incident a supply chain attack used as the delivery mechanism for a second one.

== Mandiant assesses the 3CX credentials were stolen, then used over VPN
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1078 | Valid Accounts
technique: T1133 | External Remote Services
fact: Stolen | from a personal machine, in Mandiant's assessment
fact: Used for | VPN authentication into the 3CX corporate network
fact: Gap | a VPN validating a credential without evaluating device posture
People work from personal devices, and a VPN that authenticates a password without evaluating the device it came from cannot tell the difference.
notes:
The page maps this stage to T1078, Valid Accounts, and T1133, External Remote Services. Valid Accounts means signing in with an existing account's credentials.

Mandiant assesses UNC4736 stole the employee's 3CX corporate credentials from the backdoored computer, and the actor used them to authenticate over VPN into the company network. A VPN, or virtual private network, connects a remote device into a company network.

The page calls the hop that made this possible mundane and extremely common: corporate credentials present on a personal machine, and a VPN that accepted them.

It names the same structural failure in two other kill chains: LastPass, where a DevOps engineer's home computer became the path into production, and Vercel / Context.ai, where a vendor employee's infected laptop reached a hosting platform. The page says the same failure recurs because the boundary is hard to hold.

Device posture is a device's integrity and security state. The next slide explains it, and conditional access, in general terms.

== What device posture and conditional access mean
layout: background
source: csoh.org, Zero Trust: device posture | https://csoh.org/zero-trust.html#device-posture
credit: Also from the csoh.org glossary, Conditional Access
Device posture is a device's integrity and security state. Device management tools enroll a device, apply configuration policy and attest its management state. Conditional access grants access only when conditions are met, such as a compliant device.
notes:
Background, from the csoh.org Zero Trust page and glossary, in general terms.

The Zero Trust page lists device posture among the trust signals an access decision can weigh, alongside identity provider claims and MFA strength.

Device management tools, often called MDM for mobile device management, enroll the device, apply configuration policy and attest its management state. Endpoint detection and response, or EDR, adds real-time telemetry on process behavior.

The glossary defines conditional access as policy that grants access only when contextual conditions are met, such as the device being compliant.

Also from the csoh.org glossary, Conditional Access: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-conditional-access

== The actor compromised both build environments
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Lateral Movement
technique: T1021 | Remote Services
technique: T1195.002 | Compromise Software Supply Chain
fact: Path | VPN access, then lateral movement, then build infrastructure
fact: Scope | both the Windows and macOS build environments
fact: What that indicates | a deliberate objective, not opportunistic escalation
fact: Why build systems | attacker code inherits the vendor's identity, signature and distribution at once
An organization that ran macOS clients was not safer than one running Windows.
notes:
The page maps this stage to T1021, Remote Services, and T1195.002, Compromise Software Supply Chain.

From the VPN, the actor moved laterally through 3CX's network and compromised the build environments for both the Windows and the macOS desktop applications. Lateral movement means pivoting from one foothold to other systems.

The page reads the scope as a signal. Reaching one build environment is a serious compromise. Reaching both, on two different platforms with two different toolchains, the page says, indicates deliberate and patient work toward exactly this objective rather than opportunistic escalation.

It also removed the defence customers would otherwise have had: an organization that ran macOS clients was not safer than one running Windows.

The page calls the build environment the highest-value target in any software company, for the reason SolarWinds established three years earlier: it is the one place where an attacker's code inherits the vendor's identity, signature and distribution channel in a single step.

== 3CX signed and shipped the backdoor
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Supply Chain
technique: T1553.002 | Code Signing
fact: Artifact | trojanized 3CXDesktopApp for Windows and macOS
fact: Signature | valid, and 3CX's own
fact: Customer side | normal update channel; nothing to notice at install
fact: Flagged | by EDR and antivirus vendors as early as March 22, 2023, per Volexity
Signing proves who built it, not whether their build environment was trustworthy.
notes:
The page files this stage under Supply Chain and maps it to T1553.002, Code Signing.

Trojanized 3CXDesktopApp builds went out through normal release channels, signed by 3CX, to a customer base spanning hundreds of thousands of organizations.

From the customer's side, the page says, there was nothing to notice at install. The application arrived through the ordinary update channel, carried a valid 3CX signature, and installed exactly as it always had. The page adds that EDR and antivirus vendors began flagging malicious activity from the updates as early as March 22, 2023, according to Volexity, and CrowdStrike observed the signed app beaconing to actor-controlled infrastructure on March 29. EDR is endpoint detection and response.

Every control that verifies who published software returned a clean answer, because the answer was true: 3CX really did build and sign it. The page calls this the same defeat of publisher-identity verification that Mini Shai-Hulud and Megalodon would achieve in 2026, by poisoning source rather than stealing credentials.

That is why, in the page's words, signing answers the question of whether the vendor built this, and not whether the vendor's build environment is trustworthy.

The next slide gives ATT&CK's general description of code signing.

== What code signing does, per ATT&CK
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1553.002 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1553/002/
ATT&CK says code signing gives a binary a level of authenticity from its developer. Modern Windows and macOS systems can use signing to verify software on first run, and some security policies require signed code to execute.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK files code signing under Subvert Trust Controls. It says code signing provides a level of authenticity on a binary from the developer, and a guarantee that the binary has not been tampered with. Modern Windows and macOS systems can use it to verify software on first run; Linux does not, because the platform is decentralized.

ATT&CK's entry describes adversaries obtaining code signing material to sign their own malware or tools, which yields a valid signature, unlike an invalid one. It adds that signing certificates may be used to bypass security policies that require signed code to execute.

The page's point sits beside ATT&CK's: signing establishes authorship, not integrity of the authoring process, and 3CX really did build and sign the trojanized application.

== C2 config hidden in GitHub icon files
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Command and Control
technique: T1102 | Web Service
technique: T1001.002 | Steganography
fact: Channel | .ico files fetched from a GitHub repository
fact: Concealment | base64 C2 configuration appended after valid image data
fact: Why it works | GitHub is reachable and allow-listed nearly everywhere
fact: Allow-list lesson | permitting a platform permits everything anyone hosts on it
Network detection built on destination reputation fails completely here, because the destination is legitimate.
notes:
The page maps this stage to T1102, Web Service, and T1001.002, Steganography.

The implanted downloader retrieved .ico icon files from a GitHub repository. Each carried its real payload as base64 data appended after the valid image content: command-and-control, or C2, configuration. C2 is how malware receives instructions from the people running it. Base64 writes any data as plain letters, digits and a few symbols.

The malware did not phone home to suspicious infrastructure. The page says GitHub is reachable from essentially every corporate network, is allow-listed by default in most egress policies, and serves image files constantly. Egress means outbound traffic.

Two defensive consequences follow, the page says. Network detection built on destination reputation fails completely here, because the destination is legitimate. And an allow-list that permits a whole platform permits everything hosted on it. The page calls that the same lesson as the exfiltration step in Nx / s1ngularity, where stolen secrets were pushed to public GitHub repositories using the victim's own token.

The next slide gives ATT&CK's general description of C2 over a web service.

== What ATT&CK means by C2 over a web service
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1102 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1102/
credit: Also from MITRE ATT&CK T1001.002 (attack.mitre.org)
ATT&CK says adversaries may relay data to and from a compromised system through a legitimate external web service. Hosts are likely already talking to popular sites and cloud services, which gives cover. Steganography hides data in files such as images.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK says adversaries may use an existing, legitimate external web service as a means of relaying data to and from a compromised system. Popular websites, cloud services and social media used for C2 may give a significant amount of cover, because hosts within a network are likely already communicating with them before a compromise. Web service providers commonly use SSL/TLS encryption, which gives adversaries an added level of protection. ATT&CK adds that using web services may also protect back-end C2 infrastructure from discovery through malware binary analysis.

ATT&CK's steganography entry describes hiding command and control traffic to make detection harder. Steganographic techniques hide data in digital messages passed between systems, and in some cases files with data embedded this way, such as image or document files, are used for C2.

Also from MITRE ATT&CK T1001.002: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1001/002/

== A first for Mandiant: one supply chain compromise led to another
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Impact
fact: Chain | Trading Technologies, a 3CX employee, 3CX, 3CX's customers, potentially onward
fact: Vendor risk | assessing direct suppliers says nothing about theirs
fact: Consequence | harm from a company you never heard of and could not assess
fact: Tracking | MITRE Campaign C0057
Any of those customers who ship software to others were themselves one step from becoming the next link.
notes:
The page files this stage under Impact. It maps it to no ATT&CK technique; MITRE tracks the activity as Campaign C0057.

Mandiant said this was the first time it had seen one software supply chain compromise lead to another.

Read the chain end to end, as the page does: Trading Technologies was compromised, which compromised a 3CX employee, which compromised 3CX, which compromised 3CX's customers. Any of those customers who ship software to others were themselves one step from becoming the next link.

The page says that structure defeats how vendor risk management is normally practised, because assessing your direct suppliers tells you nothing about theirs, and questionnaires do not propagate. Fourth-party risk, the page's tag for this stage, means risk that comes from your suppliers' suppliers.

It also means an organization can be materially harmed by a breach at a company it has never heard of, has no contract with, and could not have assessed.

The next slide draws the chain.

== The cascade, as the page draws it
layout: layers
layer: Trading Technologies | X_TRADER, end of life and trojanized
layer: Employee | a 3CX employee ran it on a personal computer
layer: 3CX | both build environments | a signed, backdoored app
layer: Customers | 3CX's customers, through normal update channels
layer: Onward | potentially, through customers who ship software to others
Each compromise led to the next. The last layer is potential.
notes:
This is the chain the page reads end to end, drawn top to bottom. Read the arrows as that order and nothing more.

Trading Technologies was compromised, and its end-of-life X_TRADER application had itself been trojanized. That compromised a 3CX employee, who ran it on a personal computer. That compromised 3CX, where the actor reached both build environments and 3CX signed and shipped a backdoored application. That compromised 3CX's customers, who received it through the normal update channel.

The last layer is a possibility the page raises: any of those customers who ship software to others were one step from becoming the next link. The page does not report a further link.

Mandiant called this the first cascading software supply chain compromise it was aware of.

== Signing answers who built it, not whether the build environment was trustworthy
layout: compare
col: What signing answers | Did the vendor build this? | Here, yes: 3CX built and signed the trojanized app | Every publisher check returned a clean answer, because the answer was true
col: What it does not answer | Is the vendor's build environment trustworthy? | Here, both build environments had been compromised
Researchers noted that the trojanized X_TRADER installer carried a valid signature too.
notes:
The page's summary states the pattern in one line: a supply chain attack was the delivery mechanism for a second supply chain attack.

Signing comes up twice in the chain. The malicious build was correctly signed by 3CX, and the trojanized X_TRADER installer was signed too. Every control that verifies who published software returned a clean answer, because the answer was true: 3CX really did build and sign it.

So, in the page's words, signing answers the question of whether the vendor built this, and not the question of whether the vendor's build environment is trustworthy. Signing establishes authorship, not integrity of the authoring process.

That leaves the question of what does catch a signed, trojanized application. The defenses take it up, and so does the first discussion question.

== Six defenses against six stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06
row: Break the personal-device path to the corporate network | 02
row: Treat the build environment as production | 03
row: Do not rely on code signing | 01 | 04
row: Extend vendor risk past direct suppliers | 03 | 06
row: Inventory and remove end-of-life software | 01
row: Do not trust a whole platform for egress | 05
A cell is marked only where a defense's own words cite that stage.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's six defenses against its six stages. The defenses name no stage numbers, so a cell is marked only where a defense's own words cite that stage's facts.

Breaking the personal-device path cites corporate credentials on an unmanaged machine and the VPN, stage 02. Treating the build environment as production cites both build environments reached from general network access, stage 03. Not relying on code signing cites the malicious build signed by 3CX, stage 04, and the signed X_TRADER installer, stage 01. Inventorying end-of-life software cites the unsupported entry point, stage 01. The egress defense cites the C2 channel of icon files on GitHub, stage 05.

Extending vendor risk cites a chain of unknown depth, stage 06, and its question about build-environment isolation names stage 03's gap.

The signing defense also names a behavioural signal, an application making network connections it never made before, which the page says customers noticed. It names no stage 05 fact, such as GitHub or the icon files, so the row leaves stage 05 unmarked.

Every stage has at least one tied defense.

== Close the device path, and isolate the build
layout: compare
col: Device path (stage 02) | Require device compliance for VPN and any corporate authentication | Phishing-resistant MFA bound to managed devices | A usable managed path for work otherwise done on a personal computer
col: Build environment (stage 03) | Its own network and access controls | Separate, strongly-authenticated credentials | Log and alert on every interactive login | A reproducible pipeline, so an unexplained output difference is detectable
Both build environments were reached from general network access.
notes:
Two defenses, one for where the cascade crossed and one for the build environments it reached.

First, break the personal-device-to-corporate-network path, because the page says that is where the cascade crossed. Corporate credentials on an unmanaged machine turned one trading application installed on a personal computer into a company breach. Require device compliance for VPN and any corporate authentication, so a credential alone is not sufficient from an unknown machine. Use phishing-resistant MFA bound to managed devices: multi-factor methods that cannot be relayed or replayed, such as FIDO2 security keys and passkeys. And give people a usable managed path for the work they would otherwise do on a personal computer.

Second, treat the build environment as your most sensitive production system, not as developer infrastructure. Segment build systems onto their own network with their own access controls, require separate strongly-authenticated credentials to reach them, log and alert on every interactive login, and make the pipeline reproducible so an unexplained difference in output is detectable rather than invisible.

== Look beyond the signature and direct suppliers
layout: compare
col: Signing (stages 01, 04) | Signing establishes authorship, not integrity of the authoring process | The 3CX build and the X_TRADER installer were both signed | Layer behavioural detection on top
col: Vendor risk (stages 03, 06) | Ask about build-environment isolation, supply chain, endpoint controls for staff with production access | Accept that you cannot assess a chain of unknown depth | Monitor vendor software behaviourally; be able to disable it quickly, and rehearse it
An app making network connections it never made before is the signal that survives a valid signature, the page says.
notes:
Two defenses for anyone who runs vendor software.

First, do not rely on code signing to tell you software is safe. The malicious build was correctly signed by 3CX, and the trojanized X_TRADER installer was signed too. Signing establishes authorship, not integrity of the authoring process. Layer behavioural detection on top. The page says an application that suddenly makes network connections it never made before is the signal that survives a valid signature, and that it is what customers noticed here.

Second, extend vendor risk past your direct suppliers, and accept the limits. Ask vendors about their build-environment isolation, their own software supply chain, and their endpoint controls for staff with production access; the page calls these the questions that would have surfaced this. Then accept that you cannot assess a chain of unknown depth, and design for survivability instead: monitor vendor software behaviourally, keep the ability to disable an application quickly, and rehearse it.

== Remove end-of-life software, and do not trust a whole platform
layout: compare
col: End of life (stage 01) | Unsupported software is abandoned attack surface | No patches, no advisories, no one watching the distribution channel | Find it across your estate, developer machines included, and remove it
col: Egress (stage 05) | Pair destination policy with content and behavioural inspection | Alert on binaries fetching unusual file types from code-hosting platforms | Baseline each application's normal network profile
Allow-listing a platform allow-lists everything anyone hosts there.
notes:
The last two defenses answer the first stage and the fifth.

Inventory end-of-life software and remove it. The entry point was an unsupported application still available for download years after its vendor stopped maintaining it. The page calls unsupported software abandoned attack surface in the most literal sense: no patches, no advisories, no one watching the distribution channel. Find it across your estate, including on developer machines, and remove it.

Do not treat a whole platform as a trusted egress destination. The C2 channel was icon files on GitHub. Allow-listing a platform allow-lists everything anyone hosts there, so pair destination policy with content and behavioural inspection, alert on binaries fetching unusual file types from code-hosting platforms, and baseline what each application's normal network profile looks like.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Would a valid password alone, from an unknown machine, get onto your VPN?
check: Do build systems sit on their own network, behind separate credentials?
check: Does every interactive login to a build system raise an alert?
check: Would you notice a signed app making network connections it never made before?
check: Could you disable a vendor's application quickly, and have you rehearsed it?
check: Can you find end-of-life software across your estate, developer machines included?
check: Would a binary fetching unusual file types from a code-hosting platform raise an alert?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether a valid password alone, from an unknown machine, would get onto your VPN, and whether your MFA is phishing-resistant and bound to managed devices. Ask whether your build systems sit on their own network behind separate, strongly-authenticated credentials, and whether every interactive login to them raises an alert.

Ask whether you would notice an application suddenly making network connections it never made before, even with a valid signature, and whether you could disable a vendor's application quickly, with a rehearsal behind it.

Finally, ask whether you could find end-of-life software across your estate, including developer machines, and whether a binary fetching unusual file types from a code-hosting platform would raise an alert.

== The app was validly signed. What would have flagged it?
layout: question
The trojanized 3CX app arrived through the normal update channel, carried a valid 3CX signature, and installed exactly as it always had.

Which control in your environment would have flagged it, and what would it have seen?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

The page's facts: trojanized 3CXDesktopApp builds went out through normal release channels, signed by 3CX. From the customer's side, the page says, there was nothing to notice at install. Every control that verifies who published software returned a clean answer, because the answer was true.

Ask each person to name one control in their own environment, and what it would have seen, before you show the answer slide. Watch for answers that rest on the signature or on the publisher's name.

== Behaviour is the signal that survives a valid signature
layout: answer
The page layers behavioural detection on top of signing.
- Watch for an app suddenly making network connections it never made before.
- The page says that is what customers noticed here.
- Baseline each application's normal network profile.
notes:
The page's answer is to layer behavioural detection on top of signing, because signing establishes authorship, not integrity of the authoring process.

It names the signal: an application that suddenly makes network connections it never made before. That is the signal that survives a valid signature, the page says, and it is what customers noticed here.

The page's egress defense adds two more checks: baseline what each application's normal network profile looks like, and alert on binaries fetching unusual file types from code-hosting platforms. The C2 channel here was icon files on GitHub.

Stage 04 also records that EDR and antivirus vendors began flagging malicious activity from the updates as early as March 22, 2023, per Volexity, and that CrowdStrike observed the signed app beaconing to actor-controlled infrastructure on March 29.

Bring the group's examples back to this. A control that trusted the signature got a clean answer.

== Your vendor review covered 3CX. Would it have reached X_TRADER?
layout: question
The chain ran from Trading Technologies through a 3CX employee to 3CX and its customers. Trading Technologies says it was never a software supplier to 3CX.

What would your vendor risk process have caught, and what would it miss?
notes:
This question comes from stage 06 and the vendor risk defense.

The page's facts: Trading Technologies was compromised, which compromised a 3CX employee, which compromised 3CX, which compromised 3CX's customers. At stage 01 the page says X_TRADER came from a company that, by its own account, was never a software supplier to 3CX, so no vendor risk process would ever have covered it. ReversingLabs reported that why the employee installed it remained unanswered. At stage 06 it says assessing your direct suppliers tells you nothing about theirs, and questionnaires do not propagate.

Let the group sort what a review of 3CX could have asked and what no review would have reached, before you show the answer slide.

== Ask the right questions, then design for survivability
layout: answer
The page asks for both.
- Ask about build-environment isolation, supply chain, endpoint controls for staff with production access.
- Accept that you cannot assess a chain of unknown depth.
- Monitor vendor software behaviourally; rehearse disabling it quickly.
notes:
The page's answer has two parts.

First, ask vendors about their build-environment isolation, their own software supply chain, and their endpoint controls for staff with production access. The page calls these the questions that would have surfaced this.

Second, accept that you cannot assess a chain of unknown depth, and design for survivability instead: monitor vendor software behaviourally, keep the ability to disable an application quickly, and rehearse it.

Bring the group's answers back to the page's limit. An organization can be materially harmed by a breach at a company it has never heard of, has no contract with, and could not have assessed.

