The page cites LastPass and Vercel / Context.ai at stage 02, SolarWinds at 03, Mini Shai-Hulud and Megalodon at 04, and Nx / s1ngularity at 05.
notes:
Six related kill chains on csoh.org, each one the page draws on.

At stage 02 it names LastPass, where a DevOps engineer's home computer became the path into production, and Vercel / Context.ai, where a vendor employee's infected laptop reached a hosting platform, as the same structural failure.

At stage 03 it cites SolarWinds, which established three years earlier why the build environment is the highest-value target in any software company.

At stage 04 it says Mini Shai-Hulud and Megalodon would achieve the same defeat of publisher-identity verification in 2026, by poisoning source rather than stealing credentials.

At stage 05 it calls the allow-list lesson the same as the exfiltration step in Nx / s1ngularity, where stolen secrets were pushed to public GitHub repositories using the victim's own token.

